tewise key/element order, duplicate and equality-collapse rejection\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"[1] and (1) are equal under Clojure = on every host. See README AMBIGUITIES A6.\\\"},\\n35:   {\\\"id\\\": \\\"coll/set-nan-payload-duplicate\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"encode-refusal\\\", \\\"categories\\\": [\\\"collections\\\"], \\\"input\\\": {\\\"t\\\": \\\"set\\\", \\\"items\\\": [{\\\"t\\\": \\\"float64\\\", \\\"bits\\\": \\\"7ff8000000000001\\\"}, {\\\"t\\\": \\\"float64\\\", \\\"bits\\\": \\\"7ff8000000000000\\\"}]}, \\\"hex\\\": null, \\\"sha256\\\": null, \\\"refusal\\\": \\\"duplicate-element\\\", \\\"equivalence\\\": null, \\\"distinct\\\": [], \\\"plan\\\": \\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: sorted collections, bytewise key/element order, duplicate and equality-collapse rejection\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Two NaNs with different payloads normalize to the one canonical NaN: a duplicate introduced by normalization.\\\"},\\n36:   {\\\"id\\\": \\\"coll/map-nan-payload-duplicate\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"encode-refusal\\\", \\\"categories\\\": [\\\"collections\\\"], \\\"input\\\": {\\\"t\\\": \\\"map\\\", \\\"entries\\\": [[{\\\"t\\\": \\\"float64\\\", \\\"bits\\\": \\\"7ff0000000000001\\\"}, {\\\"t\\\": \\\"int\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"1\\\"}], [{\\\"t\\\": \\\"float64\\\", \\\"bits\\\": \\\"fff8000000000000\\\"}, {\\\"t\\\": \\\"int\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"2\\\"}]]}, \\\"hex\\\": null, \\\"sha256\\\": null, \\\"refusal\\\": \\\"duplicate-key\\\", \\\"equivalence\\\": null, \\\"distinct\\\": [], \\\"plan\\\": \\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: sorted collections, bytewise key/element order, duplicate and equality-collapse rejection\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"The map-key form of the NaN normalization duplicate.\\\"},\\n37:   {\\\"id\\\": \\\"coll/map-integer-width-duplicate\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"encode-refusal\\\", \\\"categories\\\": [\\\"collections\\\"], \\\"input\\\": {\\\"t\\\": \\\"map\\\", \\\"entries\\\": [[{\\\"t\\\": \\\"int\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"1\\\"}, {\\\"t\\\": \\\"str\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"small\\\"}], [{\\\"t\\\": \\\"int\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"1\\\", \\\"host\\\": \\\"big\\\"}, {\\\"t\\\": \\\"str\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"big\\\"}]]}, \\\"hex\\\": null, \\\"sha256\\\": null, \\\"refusal\\\": \\\"duplicate-key\\\", \\\"equivalence\\\": null, \\\"distinct\\\": [], \\\"plan\\\": \\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: sorted collections, bytewise key/element order, duplicate and equality-collapse rejection; dao.jing.cbor.md Numeric identity: CBOR integers and bignum tags 2/3; host integer width is not identity\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Integer 1 held small and held big: host width is not identity, so normalization makes the two keys one encoded key.\\\"},\\n38:   {\\\"id\\\": \\\"coll/set-float-decimal-collapse\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"encode-refusal\\\", \\\"categories\\\": [\\\"collections\\\"], \\\"input\\\": {\\\"t\\\": \\\"set\\\", \\\"items\\\": [{\\\"t\\\": \\\"float64\\\", \\\"bits\\\": \\\"3ff0000000000000\\\"}, {\\\"t\\\": \\\"decimal\\\", \\\"exponent\\\": \\\"0\\\", \\\"mantissa\\\": \\\"1\\\"}]}, \\\"hex\\\": null, \\\"sha256\\\": null, \\\"refusal\\\": \\\"equality-collapse\\\", \\\"equivalence\\\": null, \\\"distinct\\\": [], \\\"plan\\\": \\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: sorted collections, bytewise key/element order, duplicate and equality-collapse rejection\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Float 1.0 and 1M are portably equal (kind ignored).\\\"},\\n39:   {\\\"id\\\": \\\"frame/vector-1-2\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"canonical\\\", \\\"categories\\\": [\\\"frames\\\", \\\"injectivity\\\"], \\\"input\\\": {\\\"t\\\": \\\"vector\\\", \\\"items\\\": [{\\\"t\\\": \\\"int\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"1\\\"}, {\\\"t\\\": \\\"int\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"2\\\"}]}, \\\"hex\\\": \\\"820102\\\", \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"94f3e3eb591c6fbe01668206677a28adc2c950a15a69f508e320af788f9a3629\\\", \\\"refusal\\\": null, \\\"equivalence\\\": null, \\\"distinct\\\": [\\\"sequence-kind/1-2\\\"], \\\"plan\\\": \\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: lists and sequences share tag 27 dao.jing/list; plain arrays are vectors\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"A plain CBOR array is a vector.\\\"},\\n40:   {\\\"id\\\": \\\"frame/list-1-2\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"canonical\\\", \\\"categories\\\": [\\\"frames\\\", \\\"injectivity\\\"], \\\"input\\\": {\\\"t\\\": \\\"list\\\", \\\"items\\\": [{\\\"t\\\": \\\"int\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"1\\\"}, {\\\"t\\\": \\\"int\\\", \\\"v\\\": \\\"2\\\"}]}, \\\"hex\\\": \\\"d81b826d64616f2e6a696e672f6c697374820102\\\", \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"fbb74bd5cb7dcc1bb176e4fc9f8116eb5e635c3edfe448679fcd0aa8d5d3155a\\\", \\\"refusal\\\": null, \\\"equivalence\\\": \\\"sequence-realization/1-2\\\", \\\"distinct\\\": [\\\"sequence-kind/1-2\\\"], \\\"plan\\\": \\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: lists and sequences share tag 27 dao.jing/list; plain arrays are vectors\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"tag 27 [\\\\\\\"dao.jing/list\\\\\\\" [1 2]].\\\"},\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"9aa50fbc-219a-49a0-98a4-a908f5e8a78f\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Interesting — `coll/map-both-slash-keywords` is a CANONICAL case with frozen hex. Note the order of keys in the hex: `a2 d81b...6161616202 d81b...63612f62 01`. Wait: the first key is `d81b827064616f2e6a696e672f6b6579776f72648261616162` = tag27 [\\\"dao.jing/keyword\\\" [\\\"a\\\" \\\"b\\\"]], and the second is `...82f663612f62` = [null \\\"a/b\\\"]. Byte-wise sort: comparing frame bytes: \\\"8261616162\\\" vs \\\"82f663612f62\\\": 0x61 ('a') < 0xf6 → [\\\"a\\\",\\\"b\\\"] comes first ✓.\\n\\nSo on Node, decoding this canonical payload refuses :host-collapse. This breaks J3 \\\"all hosts read each other's fixtures.\\\" The skip list completely skips this canonical case on Node (not even executed in groups-hold). There's a dedicated Node test that pins this refusal.\\n\\nNext, the NaN duplicate case: set-nan-payload-duplicate has bits 7ff8000000000001 and 7ff8000000000000. On JVM these are both Doubles with different raw bits. Double.equals uses doubleToLongBits, but this canonicalizes NaN to 0x7ff8000000000000! doubleToLongBits (not Raw) maps all NaNs to canonical 0x7ff8.... So Double.hashCode for both NaN payloads → same hash (doubleToLongBits-based → canonical NaN bits). And Util/equiv for doubles → Numbers/equiv → (= NaN NaN) → false? Clojure's `(= Double/NaN Double/NaN)` → false? Hmm: Clojure's = for numbers uses Numbers.equiv: `(= Double/NaN Double/NaN)` → false. But sets use .equals for key equality in the same bucket... clojure.lang.Util/equiv(Object,Object): `if (x instanceof Number) return equiv((Number)x,(Number)y)` → Numbers.equiv(double,double) → Double.compare? Numbers.equiv for two doubles: `((Number)x).doubleValue() == y`? NaN == NaN is false → equiv false → set retains both. So JVM retains both NaN payloads → refuses duplicate-element ✓. This matches report finding #3 (JVM retains them; README says \\\"host-dependent\\\").\\n\\nWait, but hash buckets: Util/hashCode(Double NaN) → Double.hashCode → doubleToLongBits → canonical → same bucket → then equiv check → false → both retained ✓.\\n\\nNode: Float64 carriers, -equiv makes all NaNs equal → merged → skip (in Node's allow set ✓... wait, the test's collapse-not-applicable node set includes set-nan-payload-duplicate and map-nan-payload-duplicate ✓).\\n\\nmap-nan-payload-duplicate: bits 7ff0000000000001 (a NaN with payload in the high bits — still NaN because exponent all-1s and fraction nonzero) and fff8000000000000 (a negative quiet NaN). Both canonicalize to 7ff8000000000000 on encode → duplicate-key. JVM: Double keys, different bits but doubleToLongBits canonicalizes → same hash; equiv is false → retained → refuses duplicate-key ✓ (finding #3: JVM retains them). Node merges → skips ✓.\\n\\nNext, checking the report's claims about Node for map-int-float-key-collapse: members are int 1 and Float64 carrier (bits 3ff0) → Node's map: key 1 (number) and Float64 → different host types → retained → codec refuses equality-collapse ✓ finding #3 (README says N/A on Node — wrong because the carrier keeps them distinct). And this id is in the test's Node allow set (never fires). OK.\\n\\nset-float-decimal-collapse Node: Float64 carrier vs Decimal carrier → retained ✓ finding #3.\\nset-signed-zero-collapse Node: Float64 0.0 vs Float64 -0.0 → -equiv uses Object.is → 0 vs -0 are different → retained ✓ finding #3 (README says N/A on Node — wrong).\\nset-int-ratio-collapse Node: 1 (number) vs Rational carrier → retained ✓.\\nset-decimal-scale-collapse Node: Decimal(0,1) vs Decimal(-1,10) → -equiv compares exponent and mantissa → different → retained ✓ finding #3.\\nset-decimal-scale-collapse JVM: BigDecimal 1M vs 1.0M — equals is false (scale differs) → retained? But the report says JVM skips set-decimal-scale-collapse as merged! Hmm. BigDecimal.hashCode: is it consistent with equals? BigDecimal(1, scale 0).hashCode vs BigDecimal(10, scale 1).hashCode: Java's BigDecimal.hashCode computes from unscaled value and scale: `hashCode = (int)(sval ... )` — different scales generally give different hashes. But: Java 8+ BigDecimal.hashCode: \\\"if scale ≤ 0...\\\" hmm. Actually BigDecimal.hashCode uses the inflated value; 1M (unscaled 1, scale 0) vs 1.0M (unscaled 10, scale 1) → different hashCodes → set retains both. But the report says JVM merged! Let me re-read the report: \\\"Skipped on JVM: num/js-unsafe-integer; coll/set-signed-zero-collapse, coll/set-decimal-scale-collapse, coll/set-vector-list-collapse, coll/map-integer-width-duplicate: host constructor merged members, which the README's N/A table permits.\\\"\\n\\nHmm — wait, but the JVM's `collapse-not-applicable` includes set-decimal-scale-collapse, and the test only skips when the count actually decreases. If BigDecimal retains both, the count doesn't decrease, it proceeds to :else → encode must refuse → does it? equality-collapse via collapse? → equiv 1M 1.0M → num= → exact keys are equal → refuse ✓ pass, not skip. Either way the test passes. The repo