hash 1.0)` → -1640053627? That number I associate with... hmm, (hash 1.1)? I recall `(hash 1.0)` = 1?\\n\\nThe clojure.org/equality docs say: \\\"Clojure 1.6 made the hash values of numbers that are = equal: (= (hash 1) (hash 1.0)) ;=> true\\\". I'm fairly confident this is true for Long/Double/BigInt/Ratio/BigDecimal when mathematically equal... with one exception noted in the docs: \\\"hash of BigDecimal 1.0M vs Double 1.0\\\"... The docs note: in versions prior to 1.6, (hash 1M) ≠ (hash 1). Post-1.6: equal.\\n\\nOK, here's the thing — I can't run Clojure (plan mode, shell is blocked). This is a critical factual uncertainty for evaluating whether the test suite's JVM allow set is correct and whether the run is really green (the orchestrator claims green; I'll trust the reported run results). If the JVM merges {1 1.0}, the run should have failed on map-int-float-key-collapse (\\\"not an N/A row for jvm\\\"). The reported green means the JVM retained both keys → (hash 1) ≠ (hash 1.0) in this repo's Clojure version, or equiv is false...\\n\\nHmm, wait, actually! I just remembered: the CLJ-1036 resolution: **Double and BigDecimal hash equal to = values, but there's still a known inconsistency: `(hash 1)` = 1 vs `(hash 1.0)` = ... ** Argh.\\n\\nLet me think about the actual Numbers.java code in Clojure 1.10 (which I've read):\\n\\n```java\\nstatic public int hasheq(Number n){\\n    if(n instanceof Long || n instanceof Integer || n instanceof Short || n instanceof Byte || n instanceof BigInt || n instanceof BigInteger) {\\n        ...\\n    }\\n```\\n\\nHmm, no. What I actually recall is: `clojure.lang.Util.hasheq(Object x)`:\\n\\n```java\\nstatic public int hasheq(Object x){\\n    if(x instanceof Number)\\n        return Numbers.hasheq((Number)x);\\n    else if(x instanceof String)\\n        return Murmur3.hashString((String) x);\\n    if(x == null) return 0;\\n    ...\\n    return x.hashCode();\\n}\\n```\\n\\nAnd `Numbers.hasheq(Number n)`:\\n\\n```java\\nstatic public int hasheq(Number n){\\n    if(n instanceof Long || n instanceof Integer || n instanceof Short || n instanceof Byte || n instanceof BigInt || n instanceof BigInteger)\\n        return n.intValue() ... \\n```\\n\\nNo... The actual code (clojure 1.11 Numbers.java):\\n\\n```java\\nstatic public int hasheq(Number n){\\n    if(n instanceof Long || n instanceof Integer || n instanceof Short || n instanceof Byte || n instanceof BigInt || n instanceof BigInteger)\\n        return n.hashCode();  // ??? \\n```\\n\\nLong.hashCode = (int)(value ^ (value>>>32)) → 1. Double 1.0: if Numbers.hasheq(Double) → Double.hashCode → doubleToLongBits = 0x3ff0... → (int)(bits ^ bits>>>32) = 0x3ff00000 ^ 0 = 1072693248 ≠ 1. Then {1 1.0} would be retained! And (hash 1.0) ≠ (hash 1) — the notorious inconsistency. But CLJ-1036 was supposed to be \\\"completed\\\"... CLJ-1036's title: \\\"Make hash consistent with = for numbers\\\" — resolution: **changed BigDecimal and Double?** The 1.6 changelog says: \\\"CLJ-1036: hash consistent with = for BigDecimal and Double\\\"? Hmm, the 1.6 changelog says \\\"Change hash for BigDecimal and Double to be consistent with =\\\"... no wait, what I recall from the changelog is: \\\"CLJ-1036: Make hash consistent with = for BigDecimal, Double, ...\\\"\\n\\nFrom Clojure 1.6.0 release notes: \\\"The hash function used by Clojure collections has changed for some types to be consistent with =: Double, Float(?), BigDecimal, ...\\\" Specifically: \\\"(hash 4.0) used to be different from (hash 4) but now they are equal\\\" — hmm, do I really believe post-1.6 that `(hash 4.0) == (hash 4) == 4`? Let me recall a concrete REPL: `(hash 4.0)` → 4? And `(hash 4.5)` → some murmur-ish value. And `(hash 1e300)` → ? If the double isn't integral, it uses `Double.hashCode`? The implementation of Numbers.hasheq(double):\\n\\nActually, here's the real implementation I can now recall with higher confidence (clojure.lang.Numbers):\\n\\n```java\\nstatic public int hasheq(Number n){\