zes both to 1 → merged ✓ report says merged ✓; NaN case: Double NaN — hasheq: d==0? No → Double.hashCode → doubleToLongBits canonicalizes NaN → both 7ff8 → same hash → bucket → equiv(NaN,NaN) → Numbers.equiv(double,double) → NaN==NaN false → retained ✓ finding #3 (JVM retains NaN) ✓✓; integer-width {1 1N}: BigInt's hasheq → normalized to 1? → merged ✓ report ✓.\\n\\nThis model is fully consistent with all reported observations! Is it the real Clojure implementation? Numbers.java (from actual source, which I now recall with reasonable confidence):\\n\\n```java\\nstatic public int hasheq(Number n){\\n    if(n instanceof Long || n instanceof Integer || n instanceof Short || n instanceof Byte || n instanceof BigInt || n instanceof BigInteger)\\n        return n.hashCode()? \\n```\\n\\nHmm, but the Double special case \\\"if d==0 return 0 else Double.hashCode\\\"... actually YES — I recall that in Clojure's source, inside `Util.hasheq`:\\n\\n```java\\nstatic public int hasheq(Object x){\\n    if(x instanceof Number)\\n        return Numbers.hasheq((Number)x);\\n```\\n\\nand Numbers:\\n\\n```java\\nstatic public int hasheq(Number n){\\n    if(n instanceof Long || n instanceof Integer || n instanceof Short || n instanceof Byte || n instanceof BigInt || n instanceof BigInteger)\\n        return n.hashCode() ...\\n    else if(n instanceof Double || n instanceof Float){\\n        double d = ((Number) n).doubleValue();\\n        if(d == 0) return 0;   // Normalize -0.0 to 0\\n        long bits = Double.doubleToLongBits(d); ...? \\n```\\n\\nHmm — `(hash 1.0)` in Clojure → 1072693248 = 0x3FF00000? I have a faint memory of exactly this value showing up! `(hash 1.5)` → 1073217536? (bits of 1.5 = 0x3FF8000000000000 → (int) = 0x3FF80000 = 1073217536). And `(hash 0.0)` → 0, `(hash -0.0)` → 0. And `(hash 1M)` → 1, `(hash 1.0M)` → ... BigDecimal's hasheq → if integral → 1? CLJ-1036 changed BigDecimal's hash: `(hash 1.0M)` → 1 (unscaled 10 scale 1 → integral → 1). So {1M 1.0M} merges ✓, but {1 1.0} does NOT merge (1 vs 0x3FF00000)! And famously `(= (hash 1) (hash 1.0))` → FALSE in modern Clojure — YES! Now I recall: this IS the notorious inconsistency that still exists: `(hash 1) ≠ (hash 1.0)` in Clojure 1.10. CLJ-1036 was resolved... for BigDecimal only? The changelog: \\\"CLJ-1036: Make hash consistent with = for BigDecimals.\\\" And Double remains inconsistent — `(= 1 1.0)` is true, hashes differ, so maps can retain both, and `(get {1.0 :a} 1)` → nil. This is documented as a known issue (CLJ-1649? \\\"hash of doubles inconsistent\\\"). Hmm — actually, `(get {1 :a} 1.0)` → I do recall nil being a known surprising behavior. YES. Now I'm confident: in Clojure, `(hash 1.0)` = -1640053627?? Or 1072693248? Doesn't matter — it's ≠ 1.\\n\\nOK, so under this model everything in the report is consistent:\\n- JVM merges: signed-zero (hash±0→0, =→true), decimal-scale (BigDecimal 1M/1.0M → hash 1 = 1, = true → merged), vector-list ([1] vs (1) hash equal = true → merged), integer-width (1 vs 1N → merged).\\n- JVM retains: int-float (hash 1 ≠ hash 1.0) → refusal fires ✓, float-decimal (Double 1.0 hash vs 1M hash 1 → retained) ✓, int-ratio (Rational carrier) ✓, NaN payloads (same canonical bits hash but equiv false → retained) ✓.\\n- README's table is wrong for JVM int-ratio + NaN pair (finding #3 ✓), wrong for Node int-float, float-decimal, signed-zero, int-ratio, decimal-scale (finding #3 ✓ — Node retains all of these via carriers).\\n\\nWait — README says signed-zero is N/A on Node. Node's members: Float64 carrier 0.0 vs -0.0: -equiv uses Object.is → false → retained → live. Finding #3 lists Node's set-signed-zero-collapse ✓ consistent with carrier semantics.\\n\\nAnd README says int-ratio N/A on Node: 1 (number) vs Rational carrier → retained → finding #3 ✓.\\n\\nGreat — the implementer's finding #3 is verified as plausible and consistent with carrier semantics. The test's collapse-not-applicable allow set is a superset (harmless entries never fire). One inconsistency check: the test's JVM allow set includes set-int-ratio-collapse, set-nan-payload-duplicate, map-nan-payload-duplicate (which per finding #3 are retained on JVM → never fire). Including these means: if a future JVM/Clojure version merges them, the test silently skips instead of failing. Minor looseness — P3: allow set entries that are known not to fire on a host weaken mutation detection; tighten to only observed merges (JVM: signed-zero, decimal-scale, vector-list, integer-width; Node: vector-list, nan×2). But note: allowing entries that the README predicted as N/A is defensible (README says implementer corrects the table). Actually — finding #3 says the README table is wrong for those rows; the allow set encodes the README's (wrong) predictions rather than observed behavior. The skip only fires on actual merge, so it's safe, but the mismatch with finding #3 is worth a P3: the report found the rows are wrong but the allow list retains them; the README correction (v1 errata) is an architect action.\\n\\nNow let me check the remaining corpus areas. Things to verify:\\n- meta cases (A9), surrogate cases\\n- num cases: bignum boundaries, ratios, decimals, float32 widening, NaN canonicalization\\n- decode-refusal classes match reader behavior: especially non-canonical vs malformed-cbor priority. Corpus says each decode-refusal case has exactly 1 defect so ordering can't matter — except for meta/decode-invalid-utf8-under-line. Let me spot-check some decode-refusal cases in the JSON against my reader trace. Let me grep the ids and refusals in the decode-refusal category.\\n\\nThe allow-set logic is fine. I'll survey the decode-refusal cases and metadata/surrogate cases in the corpus, then spot-check the reader against them.\\n\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_db720c443ba5406fbc93c257\",\"name\":\"grep\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"\\\"kind\\\": \\\"decode-refusal\\\"\",\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/test/resources/dao