ai27? Re-encode = 1bffffffffffffffff ≠ c248... → non-canonical ✓ (assuming Boring writes 1b ffff... — fixtures pin the canonical case of 2^64-1)\\n- num/bignum-empty-magnitude c240: span-big over empty = zero → value 0 → re-encode 00 → non-canonical ✓\\n- num/decimal-bignum-exponent: int-item e allow-bignum false → tag → nil → malformed-number ✓\\n- num/decimal-float-mantissa: m item is tag27 float64 frame → int-item → :tag with tag 27 → not #{2 3} → nil → malformed-number ✓. Wait — int-item on a :tag item calls `(big (item->value bs x))` only for tags 2/3; for tag 27 it returns nil (when allow-bignum? and #{2 3} tag → otherwise nil) ✓. But hmm — item->value isn't called for tag-27 there, good (otherwise it would construct a Float64 and big would throw on it).\\n- num/decimal-bignum-mantissa-in-range: m = tag2 h'01' → int-item allow-bignum true → (big (item->value ...)) → item->value of tag 2 → 1 → big → decimal [0 1] → re-encode: mantissa int-wire(1) → 01, not tag 2 → non-canonical ✓\\n- num/ratio-zero-denominator-wire: d=0 → (not (pos? (bsign d))) → malformed-number ✓\\n- num/ratio-negative-denominator: [1 -2] → malformed-number ✓\\n- num/ratio-unreduced: [2 4] → ratio reduces to 1/2 → re-encode d81e820102 ≠ input → non-canonical ✓\\n- id/tag39-*: tag 39 → identifier-tag-39 ✓ (including nesting — item->value recursion reaches there) ✓\\n- frame/list-payload-map: dao.jing/list with map payload → not :array → malformed-frame ✓\\n- frame/list-missing-payload: tag27 [name] 1-element array → pair-items → count 1 ≠ 2 → nil → destructuring nil → n nil → not :text → malformed-frame ✓\\n- frame/tag27-not-array: d81b over bare text → pair-items p (:kind :text) → nil → malformed-frame ✓\\n- frame/name-as-bytes: name is bytes → malformed-frame ✓\\n- frame/keyword-ns-integer: ns-item :uint → neither simple 22 nor text → malformed-frame ✓\\n- frame/unknown-name-*: unknown-frame-name ✓\\n- bytes/indefinite 5f41014102ff: reader concatenates → value bytes 0102 → re-encode 420102 ≠