\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: Jing's four names use tag 27 followed by exactly [name-string, payload]\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"tag 27 over [name payload extra]: the IANA flattened constructor-argument form is not Jing's shape.\\\"},\"}]},{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_bdb44af34e00499cb99237c0\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read 1/1 file, 1 line\\nNote: showing lines 60-60. More of the file remains. Use offset=61 to continue.\\n60:   {\\\"id\\\": \\\"frame/keyword-three-components\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"decode-refusal\\\", \\\"categories\\\": [\\\"frames\\\", \\\"malformed\\\"], \\\"input\\\": null, \\\"hex\\\": \\\"d81b827064616f2e6a696e672f6b6579776f726483f661616162\\\", \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"9b2748913ea05c159a2e48ddcdc9b5f5234d970d1f22de779023b5c20d472920\\\", \\\"refusal\\\": \\\"malformed-frame\\\", \\\"equivalence\\\": null, \\\"distinct\\\": [], \\\"plan\\\": \\\"dao.jing.cbor.md Encoding contract: Jing's four names use tag 27 followed by exactly [name-string, payload]\\\", \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Keyword payload with three components.\\\"},\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"16f1f41b-832a-4b94-9e08-77e82e7c0a54\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Checking the frame cases. Now let's look at the rest: the canonical cases in lines 41–114 (frame/id/meta canonical), the num canonical cases around lines 210–300, the id canonical cases around lines 180–208, and the host rejection cases. Given time constraints, I should focus on areas where the codec might deviate. Key spot checks:\\n\\n1. id/ canonical special identifiers (empty ns vs nil ns, colons, whitespace) — the codec uses `(keyword ns name)` with exact fields ✓.\\n2. num canonical cases: boundaries 2^64-1, -2^64, bignum, decimal scale, float32 widening, NaN variants, -inf.\\n3. host/ rejection cases: inst, uuid, fn, record, js-unsafe-integer, nested-in-vector.\\n\\nAlso checking `host/nested-in-vector` — on Node it's skipped (because members are char). On JVM: a vector containing char → wire_of → unsupported-value ✓.\\n\\nNext, thinking more deeply about potential codec bugs:\\n\\n**A. Encode side: `members-wire` duplicate check against `(set (map first wired))`** — hex strings; correct.\\n\\n**B. Set sorting**: `(sort-by first wired)` — first is the hex string of the key bytes; sorting hex strings in Clojure's string order = sorting bytes as unsigned ✓ (hex is lowercase, fixed 2 chars per byte, lexicographic order of lowercase hex = unsigned byte order ✓). \\\"Proper prefixes come first\\\": hex strings — a prefix of bytes is a prefix of the hex string; in string comparison, prefix < longer ✓.\\n\\n**C. Map key sorting by Boring** — fixed in fixtures.\\n\\n**D. `meta-wire` against a list**: `(meta-wire x (wire/frame 'dao.jing/list ...))` ✓ outermost layer A4.\\n\\n**E. Encode check: with-meta frame against a value whose wire node is a byte array?** Meta only applies to coll/symbol ✓.\\n\\n**F. Metadata frame contents: `(map-wire kept)`** — the metadata map itself is sorted and duplicate-checked ✓. Metadata with keys that collapse (e.g., {:a 1 \\\"a\\\" 2})? Distinct on the wire; no problem.\\n\\n**G. What about metadata on lazy seqs?** `(meta-wire x ...)` against `(or (list? x) (seq? x))` — meta of lazy-seq — OK.\\n\\n**H. Check order in `wire_of` on CLJS**: `(wire/byte-payload? x)` — Uint8Array check comes before numeric. Note: `js/Uint8Array` on CLJS — could DataView or other typed arrays sneak in? `(instance? js/Uint8Array x)` — a Uint8ClampedArray is not an instance of Uint8Array → falls through to unsupported ✓. ByteBuffer? JVM's bytes? only byte[] ✓.\\n\\n**I. JVM: `float-wire` NaN handling uses Double/isNaN ✓; raw bits are preserved for non-NaN (signed zero, infinity) ✓. JVM's Float is widened via `(double x)` in float-value ✓ exact.\\n\\nWait — float-wire takes `d` (already float-value'd) in number-wire: `(float-wire (float-value x))`. JVM's float-value = (double x) ✓.\\n\\n**J. CLJS float-wire**: `(.setFloat64 view 0 d)` → IEEE bits ✓; NaN → 0x7ff8000000000000 ✓. Note: setFloat64 of -0.0 preserves the sign ✓.\\n\\n**K. int-wire against tag 3**: `(wire/tagged 3 (magnitude-bytes (b- (b- zero b) one)))` → -1-b ✓ (b < -2^64). Example: b = -2^64-1 → magnitude 2^64 → tag 3 ✓.\\n\\n**L. b->int JVM for CBOR int range**: value -2^64: bitLength of -2^64 is 65? BigInteger(-2^64).bitLength() = 65? bitLength of -2^64: for negative numbers, bitLength = number of bits in the two's complement representation minus… bitLength of -2^64 = 64? Actually, bitLength(-2^k) = k. Hmm: bitLength of -2^64 = 64 (since it's a power of 2, so 64… no wait. (-2^64).bitLength(): bitLength of negative n = bitLength of ~n… BigInteger.valueOf(-2).bitLength()=1; -4 → 2; -2^64 → 64. So (< 64 64) is false → BigInt path ✓. For -2^64+1: bitLength 64 → BigInt. For 2^63-1: bitLength 63 → long ✓. For -2^63: bitLength 63 → long ✓ (longValue = -2^63 ✓). Good: `(< bitLength 64)` guarantees longValue is exact for both signs.\\n\\n**M. CLJS b->int: `(js/Number b)`** — Number(BigInt) rounds beyond 2^53; guarded by isSafeInteger ✓. Returns BigInt for large values ✓. But: int-wire range check is [-2^64, 2^64-1] → b->int → for values in [2^53, 2^64) → BigInt → can Boring JS handle BigInt and write major type 0/1 8-byte? Probably yes (fixed in fixture's num/int-2^64-1 canonical case). OK.\\n\\n**N. `length-of` for JVM's ai-27 arguments**: span-big returns BigInteger; `(pos? (bcmp arg max-length))` → rejects >2^53-1 as truncated. But a length in [2^53-8, 2^53-1] fits → .longValue → need bs pos n → n > remaining bytes → rejects truncated ✓.\\n\\n**O. read-head ai 24..26**: computed with reduce as a plain number ✓ (max 2^32-1).\\n\\n**P. Potential issue — `read-item`'s major 4/5 with definite n as a NUMBER from ai≤26, but n can be huge (2^32-1) → loop reads items until acc count = n; input exhausts → read-item → need → rejects truncated ✓. But acc can grow to the actual item count ≤ input bytes — bounded ✓. But: `(and n (= n (count acc)))` — if n is 4e9 and input is 4e9 bytes of 0x00… each iteration adds 1 item; 4GB input — bounded by input ✓. Memory: each item is a map {:kind :value :start :end} — roughly 100 bytes overhead per byte of input → 100× amplification! Allocation concern: a 10MB malicious payload could allocate ~1GB of item maps. RFC 8949 recommends limits. But re-encode also builds the wire tree. This is the \\\"allocation bomb\\\" concern — amplification factor is ~50–100× input size. Worth a P2/P3 note: per-item record overhead; consider a cap on total item count or input size. The plan doesn't set limits; DHT datagrams are ≤1200 bytes; file/remote can be larger. P3 note.\\n\\n**Q. Recursion depth** — flagged as P2 above. read-item and item->value recurse per nesting level; also `wire_of`/encode recursion. A malicious payload of `81818181...` (nested arrays) → depth = input size → SO at ~10k–100k frames. JVM default stack is ~512KB–1MB; each read-item frame is somewhat heavy (let bindings, closures). ~50k nesting could blow the stack. The corpus doesn't test this. P2 with suggestion: add a depth cap and reject with :malformed-cbor (or a new class) — but does changing rejection behavior require architect approval? A new rejection for out-of-corpus inputs is an implementation choice; better to flag and let the architect decide. Note: is StackOverflowError caught in tests? Not relevant.\\n\\nAlso on Node: stack overflow is RangeError — caught as an exception, but decode doesn't throw ex-info with ::refusal, it throws a raw RangeError → `refusal-of` returns [::other msg]. At the ingest boundary (step 3), a non-refusal exception could be mishandled. Same class of problem.\\n\\n**R. item->value for `:float` items — rejects with :native-float regardless of ai (25/26/27) ✓.\\n\\n**S. `:simple` items with value ≥32 from 2-byte form ✓ unsupported-simple. ai 24 with argument ≥32 ✓.\\n\\n**T. Tag dispatch with `(int tag)` in item->value** — tag is a number ≤ 2^32-1 from a 4-byte span (ai 26) → `(int tag)` CLJS truncation: (int 3000000000) → bit-or → negative — case mismatch → unknown-tag ✓. JVM: ai 27 argument → span-big's BigInteger → tag item value `(if (number? arg) arg -1)` → -1 → `(int -1)` → unknown-tag ✓. Hmm — but this means a legitimate huge tag (≥2^63) reports \\\"tag -1\\\" in the message. Cosmetic issue only.\\n\\nWait — there's actually a subtle issue: for major 6 with ai 27, read-item stores the tag as -1 (not the true value). What if the true tag = 2^64-1 = 18446744073709551615? -1 → unknown-tag ✓ correct class.\\n\\n**U. `int-item` for tags 2/3 inside decimal/ratio: `(big (item->value bs x))`** — item->value of tag 2 with bytes payload → b->int → host integer → big ✓. But note: nested bignums with leading zeros or in-range bignums pass through here without a canonicality check — the final re-encode check catches it (num/decimal-bignum-mantissa-in-range → non-canonical ✓).\\n\\n**V. Tag 4 decimal: `(decimal (b->int e) m)`** — e is a host bigint from int-item; b->int e converts back… e is already a big → (b->int big) → host integer. On JVM, if e is a BigInteger beyond long → b->int → BigInt → decimal checks scale range → out of int range → rejects with malformed-number. Hmm — canonicality class for a huge exponent: corpus A12 says bignum exponent → malformed-number; a major 0/1 integer exponent with a huge value like 2^63? → scale out of int range → malformed-number. Reasonable, not fixed in corpus but defensible. On CLJS: b->int e → BigInt → Number.isSafeInteger(BigInt) is false → rejects with malformed-number ✓.\\n\\nBut CLJS decimal: `(Decimal. exponent (big mantissa))` where exponent = (b->int e) — if e is a safe number → number exponent ✓.\\n\\nHmm — wait, there's an asymmetry: JVM `decimal` accepts any exponent that fits in Integer range (±2^31); CLJS accepts any safe integer (±2^53). A decimal with exponent 3e9 is constructible on CLJS but rejected on JVM → host-dependent ingest rejection! Cross-host divergence: a tag-4 payload with exponent 3e9 (fits in major 0 ai27, value < 2^53) → Node decodes successfully; JVM rejects with malformed-number. But the re-encode check: Node encodes decimal exponent 3e9 → int-wire → c4 [1b... mantissa] — matches input → Node accepts. JVM rejects. Host-dependent acceptance for the same canonical bytes — violates \\\"don't let the host that accepts determine this boundary\\\"! But is the corpus fixing an exponent range? A12 only fixes bignum exponent rejection. An 8-byte exponent like 3000000000: JVM BigDecimal can't hold scale > Integer.MAX_VALUE → must reject; Node Decimal carrier can. So divergence is inherent to host carriers unless J1 imposes a common upper bound. Corpus has no such case → tests pass. This is a real P2 finding: host-dependent acceptance of huge decimal exponents; minimal fix: impose a common exponent bound (e.g., reject |exponent| > some fixed limit like 2^31-1 on both hosts… JVM is already limited to int range; CLJS should adopt the same bound: reject if not in [Integer.MIN_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE]). Wait, CLJS: scale = -exponent must be in int range → exponent in [-2147483647, 2147483647]. Minimal fix: CLJS decimal checks the same 32-bit bound as JVM. Then both hosts reject identically. Note: JVM BigDecimal actually supports scales up to Integer.MAX_VALUE; no problem.\\n\\nHmm, but also: is the BigDecimal constructor `(BigDecimal. unscaledValue scale)` OK with huge scales like 2e9? Yes (lazy representation). And encode: decimal-exponent = -scale → int-wire handles negatives down to -2^64 ✓. So JVM accepts exponent -2e9, Node (with fix: reject if exponent < Integer.MIN_VALUE? -2e9 < -2147483647 → reject) — wait, Integer.MIN_VALUE = -2147483648, so -2e9 is in range ✓ both accept. Edge: exactly Integer.MIN_VALUE: JVM scale = 2147483648 > Integer.MAX_VALUE → JVM rejects (scale must be ≤ MAX)? Check: `(<= 0 (bcmp scale (big Integer/MIN_VALUE)))` and `(<= (bcmp scale (big Integer/MAX_VALUE)) 0)` → scale ∈ [MIN,MAX] → scale=2147483648 > MAX → rejects with malformed-number. CLJS with the fix should mirror: accept only exponent e where -e ∈ [Int.MIN, Int.MAX] i.e. e ∈ [-Int.MAX, -Int.MIN] = [-2147483647, 2147483648]. To be strictly symmetric, CLJS must use the same formula. OK — details for the fix.\\n\\n**W. JVM `decimal-mantissa` returns BigInteger; `big` is fine. `decimal-exponent` = (- (.scale)) — scale is int; exponent as a number ✓.\\n\\n**X. JVM `ratio` constructor: `clojure.lang.Ratio.` requires BigInteger arguments — `(bquot n g)` returns BigInteger ✓.\\n\\n**Y. `number-wire` for JVM clojure.lang.Ratio: ratio? ✓ → tag 30 [num den] ✓. But unreduced Ratio? Clojure always reduces ✓. Negative denominator? Clojure normalizes ✓.\\n\\n**Z. JVM: what if a caller passes a raw `Rational` (with denominator 2)?** Only the ratio constructor creates these, and it reduces them ✓.\\n\\n**AA. equiv between nil/booleans etc. ✓.\\n\\n**AB. `sequential-value?` includes seq? — decode produces vectors and lists ✓. But JVM: MapEntry is a vector → equiv [k v] entry ✓ no problem.\\n\\n**AC. encode of seq input: `(mapv wire-of x)` realizes the lazy seq — plan says \\\"finite lists/seqs\\\" ✓. Infinite seq → hang. Plan's domain: finite. Not a rejection issue per se; P3? Plan says \\\"finite lists/sequences\\\"; encoding an infinite seq never terminates — no guard. Minor.\\n\\n**AD. `check-text` against identifiers — namespace and name are validated ✓ (identifier-wire uses check-text). A9 covers metadata ✓. Strings ✓. What about metadata KEYS that are symbols with surrogates in their name? wire_of symbol → identifier-wire ✓.\\n\\n**AE. decode: keyword construction `(keyword ns name)`** — JVM's 2-arg keyword: does it validate/parse? `clojure.core/keyword` with 2 args: `(keyword ns name)` — creates directly via Keyword/intern with ns/name strings — hmm, actually does clojure.core/keyword with 2 args do `(Keyword. ns name ...)`? Does it parse? The 2-arg version: `(keyword nil \\\"a/b\\\")` returns a keyword with ns nil name \\\"a/b\\\" ✓ no parsing. But wait — one subtlety: JVM's `keyword` function: `([name] ...parse...) ([ns name] (Keyword/intern ns name))` — no parsing ✓. Symbol's 2-arg: `(symbol ns name)` → Symbol/create → no parsing ✓.\\n\\nBut — JVM: `(keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\")`? ns \\\"\\\" → preserves empty namespace ✓ (distinct from nil) — fixed in corpus id/ cases.\\n\\nCLJS: `(keyword ns name)` 2-arg — does it cache by fqn? `cljs.core/keyword` with 2 args → `Keyword. ns name (str ns-when-some \\\"/\\\" name)...` hmm — computes fqn. For (keyword nil \\\"a/b\\\") fqn = \\\"a/b\\\"; (keyword \\\"a\\\" \\\"b\\\") fqn = \\\"a/b\\\" → equiv equal → the known Node limitation ✓.\\n\\n**AF. decode `:text` value → `(keyword ns (:value name-item))`** — what if ns is \\\"\\\"? `(keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\")` on CLJS: hmm, 2-arg keyword with ns \\\"\\\" → does it treat \\\"\\\" as falsy? `(keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\")`: cljs keyword 2-arg: `(if (and (some? ns) ...)...)`. Actually, does cljs/keyword with ns \\\"\\\" produce ns nil? Let me think: cljs.core/keyword:\\n\\n```clojure\\n([ns name]\\n   (if (nil? ns) ... )\\n```\\nHmm. The actual cljs keyword 2-arg:\\n```clojure\\n([ns name]\\n (let [ns (if (string? ns) (munge ns true)? ...\\n```\\nI don't recall exactly. There's a subtlety: does cljs `keyword` 2-arg call `(Keyword. ns name (str (when ns (str ns \\\"/\\\")) name) nil nil)`? If ns = \\\"\\\" → fqn = \\\"/x\\\"? JVM: Keyword with ns \\\"\\\" name \\\"x\\\" → fqn \\\"/x\\\"? str = \\\":/x\\\"? Hmm wait JVM (keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\") — ns \\\"\\\" → prints as \\\":/x\\\". CLJS: `(keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\")` → fqn \\\"/x\\\"? When some? ns then `(str ns \\\"/\\\" name)` → \\\"\\\" is some? → \\\"/x\\\" ✓ same as JVM? JVM keyword's toString: \\\":\\\" + (when ns (str ns \\\"/\\\")) + name → \\\":/x\\\" ✓. And encode round-trip: namespace of that keyword → \\\"\\\" → wire [\\\"\\\", \\\"x\\\"] → re-encode matches ✓. Corpus probably has an id/keyword-empty-ns case (A8 mentions empty NAME; empty ns vs nil ns is a special case). The codec reads the namespace field directly ✓.\\n\\nHmm — but one CLJS-specific gotcha: cljs.core/keyword's cache: `(keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\")` vs later `(keyword nil \\\"/x\\\")`?? fqn collision again — same class as both-slash. Injectivity on Node is fundamentally limited by keyword interning. The corpus's special identifier cases probably include empty-ns vs nil-vs-empty: `(keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\")` vs `(keyword nil \\\"/x\\\")`? If the corpus has such a canonical case with both as keys of a map, it breaks on Node. But tests pass (only map-both-slash-keywords is skipped on Node), so other special pairs don't collide on Node… e.g.: nil vs empty ns: (keyword nil \\\"x\\\") fqn \\\"x\\\"; (keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\") fqn \\\"/x\\\" — distinct ✓. Leading colon name: (keyword nil \\\":a\\\") fqn \\\":a\\\" vs (keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"a\\\") fqn \\\"/a\\\" distinct ✓. The \\\"a/b\\\" vs ns-a-name-b collision is the one case; also (keyword \\\"a\\\" \\\"b/c\\\") fqn \\\"a/b/c\\\" vs (keyword \\\"a/b\\\" \\\"c\\\") fqn \\\"a/b/c\\\" vs (keyword nil \\\"a/b/c\\\") — does the corpus include those? map-both-slash-keywords only has the 2-entry version. A map with keys (keyword nil \\\"a/b/c\\\") + (keyword \\\"a\\\" \\\"b/c\\\") would also collide on Node — not in the corpus, same class of limitation. The :host-collapse mechanism generalizes (built check catches any merge). Good.\\n\\nAlso symbol collisions on Node: symbol equiv is field-based → no merging ✓ (previously confirmed). But wait — do CLJS symbols have fqn-based hashing?? Symbol's -hash: `hash-symbol`? If Symbol's hash uses fqn but equiv uses fields, a map could hold both (same bucket, equiv false) ✓ no problem.\\n\\n**AG. `built` count check on CLJS maps with keyword merging — ✓. But note: CLJS `(into {} pairs)` with pairs as a vector of [k v] — even if 2 pairs have the same key fqn, if they're different Keyword objects… CLJS map uses -hash and -equiv of keys → fqn hash equal, equiv equal → merges → count decreases ✓ caught.\\n\\n**AH. Now JVM: can `(into {} pairs)` merge keys that equiv distinguishes but host = merges? E.g.: pairs [[1 v1] [1.0 v2]] — can this arise from decode? check-members' collapse? rejects 1 vs 1.0 (equiv) before building ✓. So what about values that are host-= but equiv distinguishes? Is there such a pair? equiv is coarser than or equal to host =… actually not necessarily: CLJS keyword (nil \\\"a/b\\\") vs (\\\"a\\\" \\\"b\\\"): host = TRUE, equiv FALSE. That's the host-collapse direction (host merges what equiv distinguishes) ✓ caught by built. The reverse (equiv merges what host distinguishes) is caught by collapse? ✓. JVM: host = merges 1/1.0 — equiv also merges ✓ consistent. Any pair where JVM host = is true but equiv is false? BigDecimal 1M vs 1.0M: = false… wait JVM (= 1M 1.0M) → true! And equiv → num= → true ✓ same. Characters vs integers: (= \\\\a 97) false. String vs keyword false. So JVM: host= ⊆ equiv? For numbers yes. OK.\\n\\n**AI. equiv-hash JVM BigDecimal: numeric? → decimal? ✓ → num-hash ✓.\\n\\n**AJ. `collapse?` performance: O(n²) within hash groups — a 10k-key map of distinct numbers → single hash group? No — equiv-hashes are distinct → each group size 1 → `(< 1 (count group))` skips the pair loop ✓ effectively O(n). Good.\\n\\nBut — `(group-by equiv-hash xs)` on CLJS: group-by returns a map keyed by hash integers ✓.\\n\\n**AK. members-wire on encode side calls wire/encode on each key — full canonical encoding per key — O(n log n) or so, no problem.\\n\\n**AL. encode side: `wire-of` on metadata KEYS: `(map-wire kept)` — kept is a map with possibly keyword keys → identifier frame ✓; meta values are recursed ✓. Reader-position values are validated by wire-of before dissoc ✓ A9.\\n\\nHmm wait — one gap in A9 encode side: meta-wire validates the values under the 4 keys with wire-of. But what about surrogates inside the metadata map's own metadata? Rejected first (meta m) ✓ A3.\\n\\nBut a subtlety: wire-of of the value under :line — if the value is a collection with nested surrogates → recursion ✓. What if the value under :line is a map with collapsing keys? wire-of → map-wire → rejects with equality-collapse — a rejection arising from a map under a STRIPPED key. The corpus only fixes surrogates. Is rejecting equality-collapse under a stripped :line correct? \\\"Validate before stripping\\\" suggests yes (full validation). Consistent.\\n\\n**AM. decode side with-meta: `(with-meta v m)` — JVM: can v be a SYMBOL? with-meta on symbol ✓ returns a new symbol with meta. CLJS symbol ✓. But CLJS: with-meta on a set? ✓ allowed. On a list ✓.\\n\\ndecode check `(when-not (and (or (vector? v) (map? v) (set? v) (list? v) (symbol? v)) (nil? (meta v))))` — what about meta on a LAZY SEQ on the wire? decode's list frame produces `(apply list ...)` → PersistentList → list? ✓.\\n\\nWhat about with-meta around a dao.jing/list frame? v = list ✓ → (with-meta list m) ✓. Encode: meta-wire on list ✓ symmetric.\\n\\n**AN. encode: meta-wire for maps: `(meta-wire x (map-wire x))` — what if x is a RECORD? record? is rejected first ✓. What about sorted-map? → map? ✓ normalized to plain map ✓ wire map. sorted-set → set? ✓ → set-wire ✓. The corpus's sortedness equivalence groups are fixed ✓.\\n\\nBut wait — does JVM's sorted-set have meta? `(meta (sorted-set 1))` → nil ✓.\\n\\nHmm — JVM's sorted collections: `(meta-wire x ...)` calls `(meta x)` — no problem.\\n\\n**AO. JVM sorted-map with custom comparator → still map? ✓ normalized ✓ plan says \\\"normalize sorted collections to plain maps and sets\\\" ✓.\\n\\n**AP. Now Boring adapter details:**\\n\\n- `opts` def is inside the `#?(:cljd nil :default (def opts ...))` gate ✓.\\n- `frame` uses `tagged-literal` → Boring writes tag 27 [name payload]? How does Boring encode tagged literals… The frozen fixtures from J0 use tag 27 with name as a text string. Boring's tagged-literal encoding: writes `(tag 27 [(str sym) form])`? Tests pass against the frozen hex, so empirically correct.\\n- The adapter has no effect proof other than the opts map check in tests (boring-profile-options-are-locked + payloads-are-one-item-then-end + fixtures). \\\"Could a change in Boring's behavior silently change bytes without tests failing?\\\" — the frozen corpus pins all bytes; a Boring change would fail tests ✓. Index API not used ✓ (encode calls boring/encode single item). String references off — tested by absence of d90100/d819 in repetitive string payloads ✓. Note: regex `#\\\"d90100|d819\\\"` against hex — could \\\"d819\\\" appear as an accidental substring of other content? Only within that test's encoded payload; the payload is fixed ({\\\"xs\\\" [\\\"abc\\\"×3] :m ...}) — false positive would cause test failure, not a silent pass; no problem.\\n\\nHmm — one weakness: `payloads-are-one-item-then-end` only checks absence of stringref via hex regex against that one value. The entire corpus pins bytes anyway. OK.\\n\\n**AQ. Tests: decode-refusal runner** — `(doseq [c ...] (is (= refusal (refusal-of ...))))` — not wrapped in `guarded`, but refusal-of catches exceptions ✓. But what if decode throws a non-refusal error (e.g., NPE)? refusal-of catches Exception/:default → returns [::other msg] → is fails with a message ✓ (reported as failure, not crash) ✓ On JVM catches Exception — what about StackOverflowError (an Error not an Exception)? Escapes and aborts the test run. Only relevant for the depth issue.\\n\\n**AR. `groups-hold-on-produced-bytes`** — equivalence groups and distinctness groups against produced bytes ✓ injectivity.\\n\\n**AS. canonical runner: `(is (cbor/equiv v back))`** — uses equiv, metadata is ignored. Hmm — metadata: the decoded `back` has its meta reattached; equiv ignores metadata (A6). So a bug that DROPS metadata on decode wouldn't be caught by equiv! But re-encode hex comparison catches it ✓ (encode includes the meta frame). OK ✓.\\n\\n**AT. Node: `equiv v back` where v is constructed from DSL** — e.g.: int case: v = number/BigInt; back = decoded b->int → same kind ✓.\\n\\n**AU. `refusal-of` JVM only catches `Exception` — refusals are ex-info (Exception) ✓.\\n\\n**AV. encode-refusal runner: `(not= ::none built)` branch: `(is (= (keyword (get c \\\"refusal\\\")) built) id)`** — for num/ratio-zero-denominator, built = :malformed-number from the ratio constructor ✓ (report finding 4). Is this correct per the corpus? Case kind is encode-refusal with rejection class malformed-number — corpus README says malformed-number includes \\\"zero denominator at encode time\\\" ✓. Constructor throws at construction time, before encode — the test treats the constructor as part of the codec (\\\"portable constructors are part of the codec\\\") ✓ defensible; plan lists constructors as part of the codec domain. OK.\\n\\n**AW. num/js-unsafe-integer on Node**: host-value returns (js/Number v) — v is a string like \\\"9007199254740993\\\"? Number → 9007199254740992 (rounded, unsafe) → encode → host-integer? → Number.isInteger true → number-wire rejects with :unsupported-value ✓ matches corpus's expected class? Let's verify the corpus case expects \\\"unsupported-value\\\". Probably yes.\\n\\n**AX. host/character JVM: `(first v)` → char → wire_of → not string?/numeric? → :else → unsupported-value ✓. Wait — Character: is it numeric? JVM: `(numeric? \\\\a)`? host-integer? checks Long/Integer/Short/Byte/BigInt — Character not included ✓. float64? no ✓. So unsupported-value ✓.\\n\\nhost/inst: java.util.Date → not record?… Date is not a record, not a map → :else unsupported ✓. Node's js/Date → :else unsupported ✓. But wait — Node: `(js/Date. v)` — wire_of's cond: nil? no; boolean? no; string? no; keyword? no; symbol? no; byte-payload? no; numeric? — host-integer? (number? Date) false ✓; float64? no; decimal? no; ratio? no → record? — CLJS record? on js/Date → false ✓; map? false; vector? false; set? false; list?/seq? false → :else → unsupported-value ✓.\\n\\nhost/uuid Node: `(uuid v)` → CLJS UUID type → wire_of → falls to :else → unsupported-value ✓. JVM's UUID ✓.\\n\\nhost/record: Probe record → `(record? x)` → unsupported-value ✓. But wait — order: record? is checked after numeric? — Probe is not numeric ✓. JVM ✓ CLJS defrecord ✓.\\n\\nhost/fn: identity → :else unsupported ✓.\\n\\n**AY. Node's `constructible?`: `(tree-seq coll? seq value)`** — for js-unsafe-integer value 9007199254740992 (a number) — no problem. For character → not-constructible sentinel: value is ::not-constructible keyword → tree-seq on it → some = → false ✓.\\n\\nHmm — constructible? for host/nested-in-vector on Node: vector containing ::not-constructible ✓ false → `(is false \\\"not constructible here and not listed as skipped\\\")` — but it IS in the skip list (skip-by-id node contains host/character and host/nested-in-vector) → skipped before ✓. Note: skip-by-id is checked first for encode-refusal in the runner ✓. But — skip-by-id skipping is unconditional: if a case is listed but the host could have run it, it silently skips. The listed cases are genuinely impossible ✓ (char; js-unsafe-integer on JVM; both-slash on Node).\\n\\n**AZ. JVM skip list contains num/js-unsafe-integer via skip-by-id :jvm ✓.\\n\\nNow — one more important check: **`decode` returns a new byte array for :bytes — but CLJS's `copy-range` = `.slice` ✓ new; JVM's copyOfRange ✓.\\n\\n**BA. encode side byte-payload: `(copy-range x 0 (alength x))`** — copy to wire ✓ Boring then reads it… if Boring holds a reference, post-encode mutation could affect already-produced bytes? bytes are already produced ✓. Mutation isolation test ✓.\\n\\n**BB. decode's re-encode check uses `encode value`** — which could reject (e.g., decode a value that can't be re-encoded?). E.g.: a keyword with ns \\\"\\\" on Node: (keyword \\\"\\\" \\\"x\\\") → CLJS keyword with ns \\\"\\\"… encode → identifier-wire → namespace x → \\\"\\\" → wire [\\\"\\\", \\\"x\\\"] → re-encode matches ✓.\\n\\nWhat about decoding a text frame name that equals a keyword frame but has surrogates in the payload? Invalid utf8 would have rejected first ✓.\\n\\n**BC. NaN canonicality on decode**: a frame with non-canonical NaN bits, e.g. 7ff8000000000001 → float64-from-bits → JVM Double NaN (raw bits 7ff8...01) → encode: float-wire checks Double/isNaN → writes 7ff8000000000000 ≠ input → non-canonical ✓ (A13). Node ✓ same.\\n\\n**BD. Negative NaN fff8... on encode → isNaN → canonical ✓.\\n\\n**BE. -inf: bits fff0... → not NaN → written raw ✓ preserved.\\n\\n**BF. num/ canonical float cases fixed in fixtures ✓.\\n\\nNow — **CLJS `check-text` uses `(count s)`** — CLJS's count on a string → number of UTF-16 units ✓ (cljs.core/count for string = (alength s)? returns the number of code units via `-count`… in cljs `(count \\\"abc\\\")` → 3 ✓ code units (not codepoints) ✓ correct for surrogate scanning.\\n\\nJVM's `(count s)` → String.length → UTF-16 units ✓.\\n\\n**BG. `char-code` JVM `(int (.charAt ...))` ✓.\\n\\n**BH. span-key/hex — `(apply str (map ...))` on huge arrays → JVM's apply on large seqs could blow the stack (apply with huge argument lists)! `(apply str (map ...))` — str takes variadic arguments; apply on a seq of 1M elements → JVM's apply handles via toArray + invoke → StackOverflow? apply builds the argument array and calls str — str with 1M arguments → RT/str uses StringBuilder on Object[] — no problem, no stack issue (apply is a single call). OK. But bytes-hex on a 10MB byte string → 20MB string — memory heavy but no problem.\\n\\nActually wait — JVM: `(apply str huge-seq)` — clojure.core/str with many arguments → no problem. But apply's argument limit: JVM method argument limit 65535? `str` is variadic (IFn with applyTo) → apply calls .applyTo(seq) → no 64k limit ✓.\\n\\n**BI. `duplicate check` uses hex string of the full byte sequence of keys — O(bytes). No problem.\\n\\n**BJ. decode: `check-members` for maps — raw = span of KEY items only (`(map first v)`) — two keys with identical encoding → duplicate-key ✓. What about pairs where keys differ but values are identical? Not a problem ✓.\\n\\n**BK. `:map` item->value — pairs `(mapv ...)` then check-members then built. But note: item->value on keys/values recurses; nested rejections propagate ✓. Order: nested item rejections fire before duplicate check — single-defect rule ✓.\\n\\n**BL. One more: encode — `map-wire` returns a wire map `(into {} ...)` keyed by wire nodes. Boring sorts keys by encoded bytes — does Boring :canonical encode each key for sorting, or sort by in-memory comparison? If Boring sorts by encoded bytes ✓. Fixtures pin map-mixed-keys byte-by-byte ✓.\\n\\n**BM. What about wire map keys that are TaggedValues (frames, e.g., keyword keys)? Boring must sort those by encoded bytes too — fixed by coll/map-mixed-keys hex ✓ (keyword frame keys are interleaved).\\n\\n**BN. JVM's `Rational` implements IHashEq hasheq = hash [num den] — but equals is also defined; Clojure's `=` on Rational vs Rational uses equals ✓; hash consistency ✓. But Rational vs Long: (= (Rational. 1 1) 1) → Rational.equals(Long) → false; (= 1 (Rational. 1 1)) → Long path → Numbers/equiv(1, Rational)? Rational is not a Number → Util/equiv → false ✓ symmetric.\\n\\nHmm — JVM: does `Rational` implement `Comparable`? Not needed; num-compare is portable ✓. But host `compare` on Rational would throw — the plan requires portable operations to be used explicitly; dao.space integration is step 3 ✓.\\n\\n**BO. CLJS's Float64 -equiv uses Object.is — so Float64(1) vs Float64(1) ✓; NaN handling ✓. -hash: NaN → (hash \\\"nan\\\"); others → hash of hex string. Consistent with -equiv? -equiv says all NaNs are equal → same hash ✓. Object.is(0,-0) false → distinguished, hashes also differ (hex \\\"0000...\\\" vs \\\"8000...\\\") ✓ consistent.\\n\\nBut CLJS's Float64 -equiv vs plain numbers: (Float64. 1) = 1 → -equiv requires instance? → false; and (= 1 (Float64. 1)) → number vs object → false. Symmetric ✓ (both false). Asymmetric host = is exactly why the num= function exists ✓ documented.\\n\\n**BP. JVM's print-method for Rational is defined — print/parse independence: encode never prints ✓. print-method is for dev only. No problem — no print/parse in the codec path ✓. `refuse` uses `(str (type x))` in the message — message only.\\n\\nHmm — `unsupported-value` rejection's `(str (type x))` — on CLJS `(type identity)` no problem.\\n\\n**BQ. CLJS's `(js* \\\"typeof ~{}\\\" x)` for bigint? ✓.\\n\\n**BR. `big` CLJS with number x: `(js/BigInt 1.5)` throws RangeError — where could big be called with a non-integer number? `big` is called from: int-wire (b is already big), host-integer? guarded path (integer ✓), decimal-mantissa (BigInt), arg->big (number from read-head ai≤26 → integer ✓), span-big (big of byte value ✓), `decimal`'s mantissa: host integer or string ✓, `ratio`'s num/den: host integer/string ✓, float-fields fraction ✓ big of integer, bpow… no problem. `exact`: `(big x)` where x is host-integer — CLJS's host-integer? excludes non-integers ✓ but includes BigInt and safe integers ✓.\\n\\nHmm — CLJS's `exact` for host-integer: `(big x)` where x is a NUMBER like 9007199254740992 (unsafe, can't happen from decode; could happen if a caller passes it) — number-wire would reject unsafe integers at encode time, but `num=` on unsafe numbers silently converts (BigInt(9007199254740992) = rounded). num= on unsupported inputs — numeric? says host-integer? (Number.isInteger true, not -0) → yes → exact → BigInt of a rounded double. A caller comparing unsafe integers with num= gets garbage-in-garbage-out. The plan says reject unsafe integer JS Number inputs — encode does; num= doesn't. P3.\\n\\n**BS. `b->int` CLJS decode of uint items: value b (BigInt) → Number if safe, else BigInt ✓. So decoded integers are number if ≤2^53-1 ✓ symmetric with encode (number-wire of number vs BigInt produces the same bytes ✓).\\n\\n**BT. JVM's decode: b->int → Long if bitLength<64, else clojure.lang.BigInt. encode of BigInt → int-wire → big → BigInteger → same bytes ✓. Equivalence groups (integer widths) ✓.\\n\\n**BU. `equiv` of decoded JVM integers: Long vs BigInt — numeric? ✓ num= ✓.\\n\\n**BV. Now the big design question 3a: soundness of decode via own reader + re-encode. Assessment: sound, and arguably essential for correctness given Boring 0.1.30's demonstrated decoder behavior (ratio tag-30 collapse, date/UUID/stringref acceptance, JS float/integer conflation). Plan text: \\\"write these frames with tagged-literal; read as boring.data/UnknownRecord, then convert recursively via closed Jing name/shape dispatch\\\" — the architect's own work package already corrected this: \\\"Boring 0.1.30 does not reliably expose array-payload tag-27 frames as UnknownRecord; the wrapper must use :on-unknown-record to create a private tagged sentinel and then perform closed Jing name/shape dispatch.\\\" So the architect was expecting Boring decode + sentinel callback. The implementer went further: completely bypassing Boring's decoder. Is this a violation? Plan's core requirements: \\\"validate tag/name/payload shapes before lossy host conversion; successful generic decoding does not imply profile acceptance,\\\" and the rejection classes demand rejections that Boring's decoder cannot express (ratio kind loss on denominator 1 is fatal — 3/1 → 3N loses the type, and the re-encode check would produce major type 0 bytes ≠ input → non-canonical. Wait — interesting: could decode-via-Boring + re-encode check still catch that? Tag 30 [3,1] decodes to 3N via Boring → re-encode → 03 → mismatch → rejects with non-canonical. But the canonical case num/ratio/3-1 must ACCEPT and return rational kind — Boring decode returns 3N → re-encode 03 ≠ d81e820301 → canonical payload gets rejected! So Boring decode is not just risky, it's demonstrably unable to accept the frozen corpus. The implementer's choice is forced. The re-encode identity check after own-reader decode is a strong canonicality enforcer. Hidden risks: recursion depth (P2), per-item allocation overhead (P3), and double-work cost (P3/informational). Also hand-written parser correctness — pinned by 132 decode-rejection fixtures + 209 canonical cases.\\n\\nOne risk to verify: does decode reject everything outside the profile before building host values? E.g.: a native float nested deep inside a huge structure — the reader builds generic items (no host float conversion — items only store ai for :float kind ✓ no conversion), item->value rejects at :float ✓ before host conversion ✓. Tag 39 — item->value rejects ✓. String references — tags 25/256 → unknown-tag ✓. Dates — tags 0/1 → unknown-tag ✓. So there are no lossy host conversions anywhere: the generic reader holds only bytes/BigInt/text ✓. Sound.\\n\\nAlso: \\\"read as UnknownRecord\\\" — deviation documented; my recommendation: accept, architect should approve as the only viable reading given Boring 0.1.30's decoder (work package's sentinel path is also not viable, since Boring's own value mapping for tag 30 / with-meta / JS floats happens before or instead of the unknown-record callback). But the plan's sentence \\\"read as boring.data/UnknownRecord\\\" should be formally corrected.\\n\\nHidden risk of hand-written parsing: also check indefinite-length text chunk concatenation `(apply str chunks)` — no problem.\\n\\n**Another reader check — indefinite-length maps in read-item (major 5, arg nil):** loop reads pairs until break ✓; check for `(and (nil? n) (do (need bs pos 1) (= 0xff ...)))` — need returns nil or throws ✓. For definite n: reads until count acc = n ✓. Note: does `(and n (= n (count acc)))` come before the break check for nil-n? cond order: first branch checks nil-n AND break; second checks n AND count. n=0 case: immediately `(and n (= 0 0))` → empty ✓ before reading break ✓ (0xbf...ff is indefinite, not definite-0).\\n\\nEdge: definite-length map/array where n is a huge number but input is exhausted → rejects with truncated ✓.\\n\\n**Now — potential reader bug: major type 4/5 where `arg` is a NUMBER and pos has advanced — `(length-of arg pos)` — if arg comes from ai 27 → BigInteger → length-of handles ✓.\\n\\n**read-head ai 27 for major 7 → :float with ai=27 → item :float — value not read (no 8-byte consumption)? Wait: read-head ai 27 consumes 8 bytes ✓ pos advanced ✓. Item stores only ai ✓.\\n\\n**Half-precision float ai 25 consumes 2 ✓.\\n\\n**simple ai<24 → consumes nothing more ✓.\\n\\n**reader: major 6 ai 27: consumes 8 bytes for tag argument ✓.\\n\\n**reader: what about `:tag` items with huge (BigInteger) arguments — stored as -1 → unknown-tag ✓ — but wait: this could mislabel a legitimate tag ≥2^63 as -1 → unknown-tag ✓ correct class anyway.\\n\\n**Now item->value tag 27: `(let [[n payload] (pair-items p)])` — pair-items returns nil for non-array or count≠2 → destructuring nil → [nil nil] → n is nil → not :text → rejects with malformed-frame ✓ (frame/tag27-not-array, list-missing-payload, list-extra-element ✓).\\n\\n**frame->value \\\"dao.jing/list\\\": `(apply list (map ...))` — `(apply list huge)` no problem.\\n\\nNow — **what about `frame->value` case dispatch against names**: `case frame-name \\\"dao.jing/list\\\" ... (\\\"dao.jing/keyword\\\" \\\"dao.jing/symbol\\\") ...` — closed ✓ else is unknown-frame-name ✓. Case on strings — JVM's case on strings requires constants ✓ they are literals. CLJS's `case` on strings ✓ supported.\\n\\n**with-meta on CLJS list: (with-meta (apply list ...) m) ✓.\\n\\n**with-meta target check requires v to have no meta — a decoded v never has meta unless nested with-meta (which is rejected) ✓. What about with-meta on a SET? `(set? v)` ✓ allowed; encode side set meta-wire ✓ symmetric.\\n\\nNow — **encode side: can encode produce with-meta on a LAZY SEQ that has meta?** meta-wire on seq x → frame on list wire ✓; decode returns a list with meta ✓ re-encode ✓ equivalence groups fixed (probably meta/list-1-doc).\\n\\n**A7: encoding an empty realized seq → `(wire/frame 'dao.jing/list (mapv wire-of x))` → [] payload ✓ empty list frame ✓. But `(seq? x)` on empty lazy-seq ✓. What about `()` empty list → list? ✓ frame ✓ — but JVM: is `(wire_of '())` list? — yes ✓.\\n\\nHmm wait — `(or (list? x) (seq? x))` — what about JVM's `range` (lazy, unrealized)? seq? ✓.\\n\\n**wire_of for vectors: `(mapv wire-of x)` — vector's wire is vector ✓. CLJS's array? `(vector? x)` — decoded array is mapv ✓.\\n\\n**CLJS: decode produces vectors via mapv ✓; `sequential-value?` ✓.\\n\\nNow — **encode side `wire_of` for CLJS `persistent-array-map` etc. — map? ✓.\\n\\n**js objects as input? Not in the domain → :else unsupported ✓.\\n\\nNext: verify existence of `dao.jing/sha256-bytes` (tests use jing/sha256-bytes).\\n\\nAlso verify no other files changed — git status only shows those files ✓ (from context: modified test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc, untracked new files + collab docs + node_modules?!). Hmm — \\\"?? node_modules\\\" — untracked node_modules directory! Is this pre-existing? The J0 worktree probably had it too… it appears in git status; might be from a shadow-cljs build. Not part of the review deliverable but worth a P3 note: node_modules showing as untracked suggests .gitignore doesn't cover it (or was freshly created). Should I check .gitignore? Quick grep.\\n\\nAlso verify the fixtures diff — can't run git diff (shell rejected). Can I compare the committed version via read? The git object — can't run git. The fixtures file I read contains J0 parts + new helpers (lines 118–262 clearly delimited). The report claims helper additions only; the file's structure is consistent with that claim (J0 self-tests unchanged — can't diff without git, but the helper section is cleanly separated; trust it, and note the constraint).\\n\\nActually — wait, I might be able to read the git ORIG version via .git objects… not without shell. read_file on .git paths is possible but objects are zlib-compressed. Skip. Note in the review: fixture diff was verified by inspection of the current file only (helper additions are cleanly separated at lines 118–262; J0 accessors unchanged); shell rejected in plan mode so git-diff could not be run. Hmm, the overview says I can run read-only git commands — but plan mode's permission system rejected it. Note that.\\n\\nLet me verify the existence of sha256-bytes in src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me verify `dao.jing/sha256-bytes` exists and check .gitignore for node_modules, plus look at the remaining canonical numeric cases.\\n\\n\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_4b4befe598224bbe9c2b6298\",\"name\":\"grep\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"sha256-bytes\",\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc\",\"output_mode\":\"content\",\"-n\":true}},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_728ba824a1b445e2bae1b6d5\",\"name\":\"grep\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"node_modules\",\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/.gitignore\",\"output_mode\":\"content\"}},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_4c953bdb415d4b5b8cdb8938\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-jing-cbor/test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\",\"offset\":280,\"limit\":20}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1790028540577,\"messageId\":\"ec27b25d-fe5b-4559-b6db-e6c94c88dee2\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_4b4befe598224bbe9c2b6298\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:289:(defn sha256-bytes\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:291:   ClojureScript, Uint8List on Dart). `(sha256-bytes (canonical-bytes v))`\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:323:   `(sha256-bytes (canonical-bytes v))` is `(content-hash v)`. This is the\"}]},{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_728ba824a1b445e2bae1b6d5\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\".gitignore:22:node_modules/\"}]},{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_4c953bdb415d4b5