ndependence/safety: no edits to existing entry points (git status: only test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc changed, helpers section cleanly separated at lines 118-262; the rest is J0 as-committed — note that in this read-only session git-diff could not be run, shell was denied; verified by file inspection + orchestrator's status); frozen resources untouched (fixtures ns is read-only; no writer); :cljd nil gating hides entire body and Boring requires from Dart compilation; no ambient print dependency in codec code paths.\\n- Implementer's finding #3 (README N/A table errors): verified as plausible and consistent with host semantics — JVM holds set-int-ratio (Rational carrier ≠ Long under host =) and both NaN-payload cases (Double.equals/hashCode canonicalize NaN bits → same bucket, Numbers/equiv false → holds); Node holds map-int-float, float-decimal, signed-zero (Object.is distinguishes ±0.0 in carriers), int-ratio, decimal-scale, because DSL float64/decimal/ratio become carriers there, not raw JS numbers. The README rows were written from raw host number assumptions; the carriers change them. The implementer correctly did not edit the frozen README; corrections are the architect's action.\\n\\nAlso, verdict wording: READY WITH CHANGES — P2s do not break frozen bytes or corpus acceptance; they are cross-host parity + untrusted-input hardening, both fixable with small localized changes; neither blocks J1 sign-off if the architect accepts depth limit as a step 3 prerequisite… hmm, actually — should P2-2 (decimal exponent asymmetry) block? That's a real \\\"identical bytes, different acceptance\\\" divergence today. But nothing produces such payloads, and the fix is 3 lines. READY WITH CHANGES is right.\\n\\nOne more scan: is there anything the plan rejects that the codec accepts? Characters — rejected ✓. #inst/#uuid — rejected ✓ (host/ cases). Functions ✓. Records ✓. Tagged literals — encode: tagged-literal input value? `(wire_of (tagged-literal 'foo 1))` → falls to :else → unsupported ✓. decode: only 5 frame names ✓.\\n\\nAnything the plan accepts that the codec rejects? Metadata on all collection types + symbols ✓. Byte strings ✓ (encode+decode+content identity). Empty identifier names ✓ (A8). Non-characters ✓. Lazy seqs ✓. Sorted collections ✓. BigInt ✓. Decimals with huge mantissas ✓ (bignum mantissa cases pass). Ratio bignum parts ✓. Metadata maps with non-keyword keys ✓ (map-wire is generic). nil namespace ✓. I found no wrong rejections other than the JVM/Node decimal exponent window (P2-2, which is a wrong rejection only on JVM relative to Node's acceptance — the plan pins neither; the corpus is silent; flag for architect to pick the common window).\\n\\nLength: the brief requests a full review as the final response — verbosity rules say <4 lines unless the task demands detail — this task explicitly requests a structured full review. Write it structured but tight.\\n\\nNow let me write the final response. Format: must start exactly with Completed-GMT etc. Today: 2026-09-21, GMT time ~? Created 21:50 GMT. Let's say Completed-GMT: around 2026-09-21 22:35:00 GMT; local +07 → 2026-09-22 05:35:00 Indochina Time. Coding-Agent: cmd. Session-ID: pending.\\n\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-21 22:40:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 05:40:00 +07 (Indochina Time)\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n# Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES\\n\\nNo P1. The codec matches the frozen corpus semantics everywhere I could trace it (I hand-traced the reader/profile against every `mal/*`, `meta/decode-*`, `num/*` decode-refusal class and the numeric canonical boundaries). Two P2s, neither affecting frozen bytes or corpus acceptance, both small localized fixes; several P3 tightenings. Note: this session ran read-only with shell denied, so `git diff` could not be executed; the fixtures-helper claim was verified by file inspection (the J1 helper section is cleanly delimited, `cbor_fixtures.cljc:118-262`, J0 accessors above it untouched in shape) plus the orchestrator's status.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n**P2-1. No recursion-depth limit in the decode reader (unsafe-reader hardening, not a wrong-acceptance).**\\n`src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:911-991` (`read-item`) and `:1088-1139` (`item->value`) recurse per nesting level with no cap (same for `wire_of` `:812-829` on encode). An adversarial payload of ~10⁵ nested `81`-arrays or `d81b` frames overflows the host stack: JVM throws `StackOverflowError` — an `Error`, which the test's `refusal-of` (`cbor_test.cljc:70`, catches `Exception`) and any future ingress wrapper's `refusal` classification both miss — Node throws `RangeError`. Neither is an ex-info with `::refusal`. Plan sentence: decode sits at \\\"each ingress of bytes Jing did not encode itself\\\" (plan, *Layering*, lines 117-125), i.e. untrusted remote/DHT bytes by step 3/4; RFC 8949 §10 recommends nesting limits. Memory itself is input-bounded (every item consumes ≥1 byte; `length-of` `:877-885` refuses >2⁵³-1 heads as truncation; `need` checks before every copy), so this is stack-only. Smallest fix: thread a depth integer through `read-item`/`item->value` and refuse past a fixed cap (64-256; no corpus payload nests beyond ~4). The refusal class for it (frozen 16 have no depth class) is an architect choice — `:malformed-cbor` is defensible since no conforming producer emits such payloads. Must land before step 3; not a J1 byte blocker.\\n\\n**P2-2. Host-asymmetric decimal-exponent acceptance: identical canonical bytes accepted on Node, refused on JVM.**\\n`cbor.cljc:441-453`: JVM `decimal` refuses unless −exponent fits `[Integer/MIN_VALUE, Integer/MAX_VALUE]` (lines 446-449); the CLJS branch only requires `js/Number.isSafeInteger` (451-452). A canonical tag-4 payload with exponent ~10¹⁵ (< 2⁵³): Node decodes it, re-encodes identically, accepts; JVM refuses `:malformed-number` at `:1119`. That is the accepting host determining the boundary — against the plan's \\\"Do not let the accepting host determine this boundary\\\" (*Encoding contract*, line 163) — and it will trip J3's cross-host acceptance parity. The corpus pins no exponent window (A12 rules only bignum exponents out), so this is codec policy, not a corpus conflict. Smallest fix: mirror the JVM window exactly in the CLJS branch of `decimal` (refuse unless −exponent ∈ int32 range, same formula), and record the window for J2/Dart.\\n\\n**P3-1. Public carrier constructors bypass invariants; `num=` can disagree with `num-compare` for hand-built carriers.**\\n`cbor.cljc:319-365`: `(->Rational 2 4)` is publicly constructible. `num-compare` stays correct (cross-multiplication `:599-601` works on unreduced fractions), but `exact-key` `:556-563` keys on unreduced strings, so `num=` says false and `num-hash` differs for mathematically equal values — violating \\\"equal values compare zero and hash alike\\\" for that input. Decode and the `ratio` constructor (`:469-486`) always reduce, so nothing in the corpus or codec produces one; `Decimal.`/`Float64.` direct construction is benign (fields are the identity). Smallest fix: reduce in `exact`'s ratio arm (one `bgcd`), or make the deftype constructors private/validating.\\n\\n**P3-2. JVM `big` silently truncates non-integer Numbers.**\\n`cbor.cljc:101-109`: `(BigInteger/valueOf (long x))` — `(cbor/decimal 0 2.5)` or `(cbor/ratio 1.5 2)` computes garbage instead of refusing, against the plan's \\\"reject … loudly rather than computing garbage\\\" spirit (*Numeric identity*). CLJS `js/BigInt` throws natively. Fix: refuse non-integral `Number` input in the JVM arm of `big`.\\n\\n**P3-3. CLJS `num=`/`num-hash`/`num-compare` silently accept unsafe integral numbers.**\\n`cbor.cljc:380-381`: `host-integer?` admits any `Number.isInteger` number; `exact` then rounds it through `js/BigInt`, so `(num= 9007199254740992 9007199254740993)` is vacuously true on a value JS already rounded — while `encode` refuses the same input (`:745-747`, `:unsupported-value`). Plan: \\\"Reject unsafe integral JavaScript Number inputs\\\". Fix: in the CLJS arm of `exact` (not `host-integer?`, which feeds wire dispatch), throw \\\"not a portable number\\\" for integral numbers failing `isSafeInteger`.\\n\\n**P3-4. `exact-key` docstring overstates portability.**\\n`cbor.cljc:557-558` claims \\\"every host compares and hashes it the same way\\\". The key is host-independent, but its `hash` is not: JVM hashes strings with Murmur3, CLJS with the 31-based scheme, so `num-hash`/`equiv-hash` values differ cross-host for equal values. Fine for every J1/J3 requirement (hashes are only used within one host — `collapse?` grouping, host buckets), but if hash values ever cross hosts (published-index partitioning), a specified digest is needed. Fix the comment; record the limitation for the architect.\\n\\n**P3-5. Test tightening (mutation-thinking gaps).**\\n(a) `cbor_test.cljc:240`: the print-settings proof covers `(take 60 …)` of 209 canonical cases; the README obligation says \\\"the canonical cases\\\" — extend to all; cost is trivial.\\n(b) `cbor_test.cljc:50-57`: the `collapse-not-applicable` allow-sets encode the README's predicted rows, including rows the implementer's own finding 3 proved never fire (JVM: `set-int-ratio`, both NaN-payload cases; Node: `map-int-float`, `float-decimal`, `signed-zero`, `int-ratio`, `decimal-scale`). Harmless today (a skip requires an actual merge), but a future regression that starts merging — e.g. a broken `Float64 -equiv` merging ±0.0 carriers on Node — would silently skip instead of fail. Tighten to the observed merges: JVM `#{signed-zero decimal-scale vector-list integer-width}`, Node `#{vector-list set-nan map-nan}`.\\n(c) The decode-refusal runner (`:142-147`) is not `guarded`, and JVM `refusal-of` catches `Exception`, not `Throwable` — a P2-1 stack overflow would abort the whole deftest. Fold into the P2-1 fix.\\n\\n**P3-6. Housekeeping:** `node_modules` shows as untracked in git status despite `.gitignore:22` — an orchestrator tooling artifact; verify it is never committed. Not a J1 file.\\n\\n## Recommendation 3a — decoding without Boring: RATIFY\\n\\nThe Jing-owned reader is not a workaround; at Boring 0.1.30 it is the only implementation that can accept the frozen corpus. Decisive concrete case: Boring's decoder maps tag-30 `3/1` to `3N`, so canonical `num/ratio/3-1` (`d81e820301`) could never round-trip through a Boring decode — re-encoding `3N` yields `03`, and the corpus requires acceptance with rational kind retained (A11). The architect's own work package already declared the plan's \\\"read as `boring.data/UnknownRecord`\\\" sentence stale and prescribed closed dispatch; the implementer's probe results (stringref/date/UUID acceptance, with-meta mapping, `decode-seq` dropping a trailing index frame, JS float/int conflation) show the sentinel variant fails the same way, because Boring's lossy native mappings happen before any unknown-record callback. The design honors the plan's actual invariant — \\\"Validate tag/name/payload shapes before any lossy host conversion\\\" — literally: the generic reader holds only bytes/BigInt/text items and refuses `:float` items (`:1093`) without ever converting their bits; Boring remains the sole byte writer; the re-encode identity check (`:1154`) enforces shortest heads, ordering, reduced ratios, canonical NaN, and metadata omission by construction, all pinned by 132 decode-refusal + 209 canonical fixtures, so a Boring writer behavior change fails tests rather than silently altering bytes. Hidden risks of hand-parsing: stack depth (P2-1, fix before step 3) and per-item allocation overhead (each item is a map; ~constant×input amplification — bounded, but worth an ingress sizing note for remote/DHT). Conditions: ratify with the depth-limit fix, a formal plan correction of the decode-path sentence, and the allocation note.\\n\\n## Recommendation 3b — `:host-collapse` on Node: KEEP THE REFUSAL, RATIFY A NEW CLASS\\n\\nRefusing is unambiguously right: the two keywords are *not* equal under Jing's portable equality (`equiv` compares `[kind namespace name]` fields, `:605-607`), the bytes are canonical and distinct, and \\\"losing an entry is never acceptable\\\". No frozen class fits honestly — `:equality-collapse` would assert portable equality that does not exist; `:duplicate-key`, `:non-canonical`, `:malformed-cbor` are all false about these bytes. So: a 17th class, ratified by architect ruling as a README erratum (the frozen file stays unedited; the implementer correctly did not touch it). This does not contradict \\\"do not let the accepting host determine this boundary\\\": that sentence governs Jing's *acceptance rules*, which are host-independent here (raw-duplicate and portable-collapse checks run identically everywhere, and the decode-side twins `mal/collapse-*` enforce on every host); CLJS keyword interning (`-equiv` on the joined `fqn`) is a host data-model limit no acceptance rule can cure — Node cannot hold the value at all. Scope of the limit: CLJS **map keys and set elements** with colliding joined names only (`(keyword nil \\\"a/b\\\")`/`(keyword \\\"a\\\" \\\"b\\\")`, and analogously `(keyword \\\"a\\\" \\\"b/c\\\")`/`(keyword \\\"a/b\\\" \\\"c\\\")`/`(keyword nil \\\"a/b/c\\\")`); CLJS symbols are unaffected (`Symbol -equiv` is field-based), keyword *values* in vectors/lists are unaffected (no merging), JVM and — to verify in J2 — Dart are unaffected. Consequence to record for J3: `coll/map-both-slash-keywords` cannot round-trip on Node; the by-id skip plus the evidence test (`cbor_test.cljc:170-178`) is the right pin. If cross-host fidelity for this identifier class ever becomes a requirement, the fix is a CLJS keyword carrier in a future contract version — not a v1 change.\\n\\n## Checked and clean\\n\\n- **Ordering/duplicates before construction:** maps sorted by Boring under `:canonical` (pinned by `coll/map-mixed-keys`/`-reversed` sharing one hex, bytewise not length-first, incl. the `mal/map-keys-length-first` twin); sets self-sorted by lowercase-hex of encoded elements = unsigned bytewise, proper-prefix-first (`set-wire` `:781-784`); raw-byte duplicates and portable collapses refused before `(into {})`/`(into #{})` on encode (`members-wire` `:756-771`) and before construction on decode (`check-members` `:1013-1021`, `built` `:1024-1031`). Wire-node map keys can only host-merge when their bytes are equal, which the duplicate check refuses first — no silent entry loss.\\n- **UTF-8/surrogates:** strict decoders both hosts (`utf8` `:888-903`); encode-side surrogate scan over all text, identifier components, and metadata values under stripped keys (A9 validate-before-strip, `meta-wire` `:797-799`); decode-side `:invalid-utf8` fires during the structural read, before strip/canonicality — traced `meta/decode-invalid-utf8-under-line` (ED A0 80 under `:line`) exactly.\\n- **Identifiers:** two-arg `keyword`/`symbol` from fields on decode (`:1059-1062`), `namespace`/`name` on encode — no print/parse anywhere in codec paths (`pr-str` only in test messages; the JVM `print-method` is diagnostic); tag 39 refused unconditionally incl. nested (`id/tag39-map-key`, `-in-vector` traced).\\n- **Frames/metadata:** exact `[name payload]` via `pair-items`; closed five-name dispatch; all malformed arity/shape/name-bytes cases traced to `:malformed-frame`/`:unknown-frame-name`; A4 outermost with-meta; A5 strip scope (unqualified four keys, top level of every metadata map); P2-1/A3 bare-meta-map enforced on both sides (`:795-796`, `:1078-1079`); nested with-meta, meta-on-string/keyword/integer refusals traced.\\n- **Floats:** native float items carry only the ai, never converted, refused (`:1093`, `num/native-*` incl. half-float and in-vector); float32 widened exactly (`(double Float)` / `getFloat32`); every NaN → `7ff8000000000000` incl. signaling/negative/float32 NaNs; signed zeros and infinities raw-bit preserved; A13 decode of non-canonical NaN bits → `:non-canonical` via re-encode.\\n- **Numeric identity:** exact reduced-triple model — no double rounding anywhere (cross-multiplication compare, positive denominators guaranteed on every path incl. subnormals and ±0.0); native/carrier symmetry in both operand orders; −∞ < finite < +∞ < NaN with all NaNs equal; `Rational` carrier for denominator 1 on JVM (`:483-486`, since `clojure.lang.Ratio` cannot hold it); BigInt/long normalized by value; CLJS safe-integer/BigInt split. I attempted counterexamples for hash consistency and compare transitivity: none reachable through public constructors or decode — only via direct unreduced carrier construction (P3-1).\\n- **Boring adapter/effect proofs:** fixed `opts` pinned by test; stringref/shapes overrides throw under `:canonical` (pinned); ordinary one-item `boring/encode` only — the adapter never touches the decoder or indexed API; stringref absence pinned by hex check and by every frozen byte; trailing second item and index-like frame refused `:trailing-data`. A Boring byte-behavior change fails the corpus, not silently.\\n- **Tests:** refusal classes matched exactly per case; `guarded` runners; skips by id with printed reason; conditional N/A skips fire only on an actual host merge (otherwise hard failure) — tight; decode-side collapse twins run on all hosts, so encode-side host-merge N/As don't weaken the rule; equivalence/distinction groups re-verified against produced bytes; byte-isolation covers input, decoded, and payload mutation; print-independence covered (partially — P3-5a).\\n- **Implementer finding 3 (README N/A-table errors) — confirmed:** JVM holds `set-int-ratio` (Rational carrier is not `=` to Long) and both NaN-payload cases (Double hashing canonicalizes NaN bits → same bucket, `Numbers/equiv` false → held); Node holds `map-int-float`, `float-decimal`, `signed-zero` (carrier `-equiv` uses `Object.is`), `int-ratio`, `decimal-scale`, because the DSL builds carriers, not raw JS numbers. The README rows assumed raw host numbers; the table is wrong, the bytes are not. README correction is an architect action (frozen file).\\n- **Independence/safety:** no existing entry point edited (git status scope: only `test/dao/jing/cbor_fixtures.cljc` modified, helpers only); frozen resource never written (loader is read-only by construction); `:cljd nil` gating hides the entire body and the Boring require from the Dart compile; no ambient print dependence in any codec path; `dao.jing/sha256-bytes` exists (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:289`).\"}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1790029371425,\"messageId\":\"ea90e49b-4354-43c0-8cef-a4b51a02889d\"}}}}}}}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2080,2300p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc; rg -n \\\"\"'^(def|deftest)|closure|totals|per-lane|shared\" test/yang/host src/dev | head -n 150; uptime; git status --short'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '2080,2300p' src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc; rg -n \\\"\"'^(def|deftest)|closure|totals|per-lane|shared\" test/yang/host src/dev | head -n 150; uptime; git status --short'","aggregated_output":"   after install (docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6's module shape).\"\n  '#{spec body})\n\n\n(defn- validate-module-name!\n  [name]\n  (when (or (empty? name)\n            (str/starts-with? name \".\")\n            (str/ends-with? name \".\")\n            (str/includes? name \"..\")\n            (str/includes? name \"$\"))\n    (throw (ex-info \"a module name is dotted identifiers\"\n                    {:yang.python.antlr/diagnostic :yang.python.antlr/unsupported,\n                     :construct \"malformed module name\",\n                     :name name}))))\n\n\n(defn module-packet\n  \"One ok packet as the linker module `pym.<mangled>`: one wide\n   application whose operands are `(require 'py)`, then the spec and\n   body definitions. Guest imports deliver at statement time; module-spec\n   still pins every static import. `name` is\n   the dotted Python module name and `package?` (default false) whether\n   the unit is a package, which is source layout, not derivable from\n   rows: the spec records both, the runtime derives `__package__` from\n   them, and the lowering resolves relative imports against them. The\n   body is the same form `lower-packet` runs as `__main__`: one code\n   image, two roles.\"\n  [packet {:keys [name package?]}]\n  (validate-module-name! name)\n  (let [unit {:name name, :package? (boolean package?)}\n        defs [(u/def! 'spec (u/lit unit))\n              (u/def! 'body (u/lam [globals-sym globals-fn-sym]\n                                   (lower-module-body packet unit)))]\n        operands (into [(require-node prelude/module-name)] defs)\n        params (mapv (fn [i] (symbol (str \"%d\" i))) (range (count operands)))]\n    (u/mark-tails (apply u/app (u/lam params (u/lit nil)) operands))))\n\n\n(defn- declare-free-name\n  \"The `:primitives` entry free name `n` declares over `registry`, or nil\n   when `covered` (the requirement-pinned module names) covers it.\"\n  [registry covered n]\n  (core-module/declare-free-name :yang.python.antlr/refusal\n                                 :yang.python.antlr/undeclared-free\n                                 registry covered n))\n\n\n(defn primitive-declaration\n  \"The `:primitives` profile free name `n` declares over `registry` (a bare\n   primitive's `vm/primitives` profile, or a host export's), as\n   `module-spec` declares it; throws on an undeclared name.\"\n  [registry n]\n  (second (declare-free-name registry #{} n)))\n\n\n(defn module-spec\n  \"`yin.vm.linker.publish/publish-module!`'s spec for the Python module\n   `name` over the host registry `registry`, requiring `py` at\n   `py-address` and each imported module at its manifest address in\n   `deps` ({python-name address}). A free name of the module tree is\n   declared one of three ways, as the hook prelude's emitter declares\n   its own: a name qualified by `py` or by an imported `pym.<i>` is\n   covered by that requirement, discharged by a linked module of equal\n   manifest address; a bare name is a primitive declared by its\n   `vm/primitives` profile; any other `ns/name` is a host export\n   declared by the profile address the registry's host module `ns`\n   publishes. A free name none of these supplies is refused before\n   anything is published. `package?` is `module-packet`'s. This\n   namespace emits; it never publishes.\"\n  [registry packet {:keys [name package? py-address deps]}]\n  (core-module/module-spec\n    :yang.python.antlr/refusal :yang.python.antlr/undeclared-free registry\n    {:name (module-symbol name),\n     :ast (module-packet packet {:name name, :package? package?}),\n     :exports module-exports,\n     :runtime-name prelude/module-name,\n     :runtime-address py-address,\n     :deps (into {} (map (fn [[n a]] [(module-symbol n) a])) deps)}))\n\n\n(def ^:private module-tree core-module/module-tree)\n\n\n(def ^:private initializing-key :yang.python.antlr/initializing)\n\n\n(defn- cyclic-import-from\n  \"Preserve IMPORT_FROM's attribute/submodule fallback, specializing its\n   failure only while the target's task-local module object is initializing.\"\n  [m n]\n  (u/let1 '%from-module m\n          (u/let1 '%from-name n\n                  (u/let1 '%from-result (app* 'cell/new none)\n                          (app* 'py/try\n                                (u/lam [] (app* 'cell/set! (u/v '%from-result)\n                                                (app* 'py/import-from (u/v '%from-module) (u/v '%from-name))))\n                                (u/lam ['%from-error]\n                                       (u/if-node\n                                         (app* 'get (app* 'cell/get (u/v '%from-module))\n                                               (u/lit initializing-key) (u/lit false))\n                                         (app* 'py/import-fail\n                                               (app* 'py/str\n                                                     (app* 'data/str-concat\n                                                           (u/lit \"cannot import name '\")\n                                                           (app* 'get (u/v '%from-name) (u/lit :py/str))\n                                                           (u/lit \"' from partially initialized module '\")\n                                                           (app* 'get (app* 'py/lookup-attr (u/v '%from-module)\n                                                                            (u/lit \"__name__\")) (u/lit :py/str))\n                                                           (u/lit \"' (most likely due to a circular import)\"))))\n                                         (app* 'py/raise (u/v '%from-error))))\n                                (u/lam [] (app* 'cell/get (u/v '%from-result))))))))\n\n\n(defn scc-module-specs\n  \"Canonical I5 construction: sorted members, indexed bare exports\n   m0-spec/m0-body, etc.; one pyscc.<mangled-members> bundle followed by\n   aliases in member order. Internal deliveries read those exports without\n   requiring aliases. Only py and outgoing imports are pinned by the bundle.\n   Alias :requires is filled with the bundle address after publication.\"\n  [registry units py-address deps]\n  (doseq [{:keys [name]} units]\n    (validate-module-name! name))\n  (let [units (vec (sort-by :name units))\n        deps (apply dissoc deps (map :name units))\n        bundle-name (symbol (str \"pyscc.\" (str/join \"$$\" (map #(str/replace (:name %) \".\" \"$\") units))))\n        keys-for (into {} (map-indexed\n                            (fn [i unit]\n                              [(:name unit) [(symbol (str \"m\" i \"-spec\"))\n                                             (symbol (str \"m\" i \"-body\"))]]) units))\n        units-by-name (into {} (map (fn [u] [(:name u) u]) units))\n        internal-names (into {} (map (fn [nm] [(module-symbol nm) nm])) (keys keys-for))\n        internal (set (keys internal-names))\n        rewrite (fn [node]\n                  (cond\n                    (and (= :variable (:type node))\n                         (symbol? (:name node))\n                         (some? (namespace (:name node)))\n                         (contains? internal (symbol (namespace (:name node))))\n                         (= \"spec\" (name (:name node))))\n                    (let [nm (get internal-names (symbol (namespace (:name node))))\n                          u (get units-by-name nm)]\n                      (u/lit {:name nm, :package? (boolean (:package? u))}))\n                    (and (= :variable (:type node))\n                         (symbol? (:name node))\n                         (some? (namespace (:name node)))\n                         (contains? internal (symbol (namespace (:name node))))\n                         (= \"body\" (name (:name node))))\n                    (let [nm (get internal-names (symbol (namespace (:name node))))\n                          [_ body-key] (get keys-for nm)]\n                      (u/v body-key))\n                    (and (= :application (:type node))\n                         (= 'try-require (get-in node [:operator :name]))\n                         (contains? internal (get-in node [:operands 0 :value])))\n                    (u/lit {:status :ok})\n                    (and (= :application (:type node))\n                         (= 'py/import-from (get-in node [:operator :name])))\n                    (apply cyclic-import-from (:operands node))\n                    :else node))\n        wrapped-bodies\n        (mapv (fn [{:keys [name packet package?]}]\n                (let [unit {:name name, :package? (boolean package?)}\n                      body (walk/postwalk rewrite (lower-module-body packet unit))\n                      flag (fn [v]\n                             (app* 'cell/set! (u/v '%module)\n                                   (app* 'assoc (app* 'cell/get (u/v '%module))\n                                         (u/lit initializing-key) (u/lit v))))]\n                  (u/lam [globals-sym globals-fn-sym]\n                         (u/let1 '%module (app* 'py/modules-ref (u/lit {:py/str name}))\n                                 (u/then (flag true)\n                                         (app* 'py/try-finally (u/lam [] body)\n                                               (u/lam [u/discard] (flag false))))))))\n              units)\n        spec-defs\n        (mapcat (fn [{:keys [name package?]}]\n                  (let [[spec-key _] (get keys-for name)\n                        unit {:name name, :package? (boolean package?)}]\n                    [(u/def! spec-key (u/lit unit))]))\n                units)\n        body-defs\n        (map (fn [{:keys [name]} body]\n               (let [[_ body-key] (get keys-for name)]\n                 (u/def! body-key body)))\n             units wrapped-bodies)\n        ;; The linker discharges body reads only when their definitions\n        ;; precede every module-level application site. Install the pure\n        ;; sibling bindings before require; defining the lambdas runs no py.\n        ast (module-tree (concat spec-defs\n                                 body-defs\n                                 [(require-node prelude/module-name)]))\n        exports (set (mapcat val keys-for))\n        bundle (core-module/module-spec\n                 :yang.python.antlr/refusal :yang.python.antlr/undeclared-free\n                 registry\n                 {:name bundle-name,\n                  :ast ast,\n                  :exports exports,\n                  :runtime-name prelude/module-name,\n                  :runtime-address py-address,\n                  :deps (into {} (map (fn [[n a]] [(module-symbol n) a])) deps)})\n        require-profile (vm/profile-of vm/primitives 'require)]\n    (core-module/scc-module-specs\n      bundle\n      (mapv (fn [{:keys [name]}]\n              (let [[sk bk] (get keys-for name)]\n                (core-module/alias-spec (module-symbol name) module-exports\n                                        require-profile bundle-name sk bk)))\n            units))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The lowering stage\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def program-medium\n  \"The program medium's logical identity when the composition names none.\"\n  :yang.python.antlr/program)\n\n\n(def ^:private stage-config\n  {:diagnostic-ns diagnostic-ns,\nrg: test/yang/host: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nsrc/dev/jolt_json_shim_check.cljc:9:;; (this is a jolt dev file, not shared code).\nsrc/dev/jolt_j0c_stack_child.clj:8:;; the shared exporter); native calls jolt_parser_expo